  "worked_solution": "Since the function and its first and second-order partial derivatives are continuous on an open set $U,$ the mixed partial derivatives must be equal, that is, ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y).$ Let's check if this condition is true for each of our pairs of first-order partial derivatives.\n\n- For the first pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(2x{y}^{2}-y)=4xy-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(2{x}^{2}y-2x)=4xy-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)\\ne {f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the first pair cannot represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the second pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(yx{e}^{x}+{y}^{2})=x{e}^{x}+2y \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}((x-1){e}^{x}+2xy)=x{e}^{x}+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$, the second pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\n- For the third pair, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{xy}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202y}(x-y)=-1 \\\\ {f}_{yx}(x,y) & =\\frac{\u2202}{\u2202x}(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061y}-x)=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${f}_{xy}(x,y)={f}_{yx}(x,y)$,  the third pair could represent the first-order partial derivatives of $f(x,y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is 'II and III only'.",
